Original commit body from 25295db3ef3668b76fd31c7bbf77b415de0ca293:

python.yaml rowed platform's host-description functions under
host_info_read only. Measured on CPython 3.12 / Linux by wrapping
subprocess.check_output, three of them also start a child:
architecture() runs `file -b <sys.executable>` (_syscmd_file),
processor() runs `uname -p` (_Processor.from_subprocess, the fallback on
every platform but win32 and OpenVMS), and platform() runs it through
uname().processor. The other rowed functions spawn nothing.

The three are split into their own row, declared simultaneous under
host_info_read AND subprocess (one call, both crossings, no argument that
decides between them), and added to HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES under that set's
own rule (a launch is a launch; builtins.help). The platform
module_completeness note said "rowed under env_read", stale since the
INV-tutar split; corrected.

No measured corpus effect on the self-survey, which calls none of the
three (platform.system / machine / python_version only). This is a
correctness fix, not claimed as recall.

Addresses INV-bosus.

